{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789466473,"nonce":"wpiTl1nRGurNSPiAvEG6X5bYdwE=","message":"Scoped down after reading the LLVM backend: it models a struct value as a first-class LLVM aggregate throughout (phis of struct type, insertvalue chains, extractvalue), Cranelift as a pointer to storage; moving LLVM to the pointer model is a rewrite of every aggregate path. The step that unblocks per-function promotion is smaller: (1) one description of a function's convention from its HIR signature (crates/compiler/src/abi.rs: which parameters travel by pointer, whether the return is direct, a header pointer, or through a leading destination), derived from Cranelift's rules and used by Cranelift's translate_signature_for unchanged in effect; (2) LLVM call sites to functions outside its module follow that description (alloca + store + pass the pointer for an aggregate argument, alloca destination + load for an aggregate return); (3) LLVM promotion is admitted only for a function whose own signature has no by-value aggregate, since its entry is published into a bead Cranelift callers use. Full unification of the LLVM aggregate model stays owed under this issue after that.","files":null}]}